1 TPU
  TPU is a lightweight text editor in the spirit of DEC TPU/EVE.
  Text lives in buffers; a file is only an optional attribute of a
  buffer. Commands are typed after the DO key (KP7 or
  GOLD-Enter) at the "Command:" prompt, any unambiguous abbreviation
  will do: "GET F foo.c", "SH B". GOLD is KP/ or Esc: press it, then
  the next key. F1 is HELP. EXIT (Ctrl-Z) writes and leaves, QUIT
  (GOLD-Q) leaves.
  Every key is journaled into <file>.tjl; -recover replays it.

  At the "Topic?" prompt below type a topic, Return alone leaves.
  HELP KEYS or SHOW KEYS (GOLD-K) lists the live key bindings, HELP
  KEYPAD draws the keypad, and HELP PC KEYPAD explains a keypad
  without application mode.

1 BUFFER
  BUFFER name

  Switch the current window to the named buffer. If there is no such
  buffer an empty one is created, not tied to any file: a scratch pad.
  BUFFER without a name goes back to the previous buffer of the window.
  Also: GOLD-B.

1 GET FILE
  GET FILE filename

  Read a file into a buffer named after the file and show it. If the
  file does not exist the buffer is empty and the file is created on
  EXIT or WRITE FILE. Also: GOLD-G.

1 WRITE FILE
  WRITE FILE [filename]

  Write the current buffer to its file, or to the named file, which
  then becomes the buffer's file. Also: GOLD-W.

1 INCLUDE FILE
  INCLUDE FILE filename

  Insert the content of a file at the cursor. Also: GOLD-I.

1 SHOW BUFFERS
  List all buffers with their size, file and state. Press Return to
  come back. Also: GOLD-KP7, GOLD-S.

1 DELETE BUFFER
  DELETE BUFFER name

  Remove a buffer. A modified buffer asks for confirmation. System
  buffers cannot be deleted.

1 EXIT
  Write every modified buffer that has a file, ask about the others,
  and leave. Keys: Ctrl-Z, GOLD-X.

1 QUIT
  Leave without writing anything; asks for confirmation if something
  was modified. Keys: GOLD-Q.

1 FIND
  FIND string

  Search in the current direction (see FORWARD and REVERSE). A pattern
  typed in lower case matches regardless of case, in any script the
  locale knows (LANG=C.UTF-8 covers Cyrillic); a pattern with an
  upper-case letter matches exactly. Keys: Find, GOLD-F.

1 FIND NEXT
  Search again for the last string. Keys: KP*, or Find with an
  empty answer.

1 REPLACE
  REPLACE old

  Prompts for the new string, then stops at each match and asks:
  Yes, No, All, Last or Quit. Keys: GOLD-KP1, GOLD-Find, GOLD-R.

1 SELECT
  Mark the start of a selection at the cursor, then move: the selected
  text is shown in reverse video. Pressing
  SELECT again cancels it, as does RESET. Keys: Select, KP., and the 5
  of a PC keypad (Begin).

1 REMOVE
  Cut the selection into the INSERT HERE buffer. Keys: Remove, KP6.

1 STORE TEXT
  Copy the selection into the INSERT HERE buffer without deleting it.
  Keys: GOLD-Remove, GOLD-KP9.

1 INSERT HERE
  Insert the INSERT HERE buffer at the cursor. Keys: Insert Here, KP9,
  GOLD-KP6.

1 RESTORE
  Insert back the text erased by the last ERASE WORD, ERASE LINE or
  ERASE START OF LINE. Keys: GOLD-KP-, GOLD-KP+, GOLD-KP,.

1 LEARN
  Start recording every key you press. Finish with Ctrl-R (REMEMBER),
  which asks for a key to bind the sequence to. Keys are recorded, not
  commands, so prompts and typed text are replayed exactly. A failing
  command (for example a FIND that finds nothing) stops the replay,
  which is how "REPEAT 999 <macro key>" walks through a whole buffer.
  Key: Ctrl-K.

1 REMEMBER
  Stop recording a learned sequence and bind it to a key. Key: Ctrl-R.

1 REPEAT
  REPEAT count

  Repeat the next key that many times. GOLD followed by digits and a
  key does the same thing, as in EDT.

1 DEFINE KEY
  DEFINE KEY command

  Bind a command to a key. Also: GOLD-D. Use SHOW KEYS (GOLD-K) to
  see the current bindings, SAVE KEYS to keep them for the next start.

1 TWO WINDOWS
  Split the screen; both windows show the current buffer at first.
  OTHER WINDOW (GOLD-O) moves between them, ONE WINDOW (GOLD-Z)
  goes back. Keys: GOLD-T.

1 LINE
  LINE number

  Go to a line. Keys: GOLD-KP0, GOLD-L.

1 MOVE BY LINE
  Move to the end of the line going forward, or to the start going in
  reverse; at the end of a line it steps to the next one. Key: KP0.

1 MOVE BY CHAR
  Move one character in the current direction, over line ends too, as
  EDT's CHAR. Key: KP3.

1 MOVE BY WORD
  Move one word in the current direction. Key: KP1.

1 NEXT WORD
  Move one word forward whatever the direction is. Key: GOLD-Right.

1 PREVIOUS WORD
  Move one word back whatever the direction is. Key: GOLD-Left.

1 KEYPAD
  The keypad in application mode, laid out the way EDT emulators put
  the VT keypad on a PC: the PF row on NumLock / * -, here moved off
  NumLock (a terminal never sends it) and HELP moved to F1. The keys
  do what EDT's do where TPU has the command: - erases a line, 3
  moves a character, 7 is DO (EDT's GOLD-7 COMMAND).

      NumLock    /          *          -
      (none)     GOLD       FIND NEXT  ERASE LINE
                            G: FIND    G: RESTORE
      7          8          9          +
      DO         NEXT SCR   INS HERE   ERASE CHAR
      G: SH BUF  G: PREV SC G: STORE   G: RESTORE
      4          5          6
      FORWARD    REVERSE    REMOVE     Enter
      G: BOTTOM  G: TOP     G: INS HR  RETURN
      1          2          3          G: DO
      WORD       END OF LN  CHAR
      G: REPLACE G: ERASE L
      0                     .
      MOVE BY LINE          SELECT
      G: LINE               G: RESET

  F1 is HELP, GOLD-F1 SHOW KEYS. If / and * type text instead, your
  terminal ignores application mode: see HELP PC KEYPAD.

1 PC KEYPAD
  Most PC terminal emulators never switch the keypad to application
  mode: with NumLock off the digits send the cursor block codes, with
  NumLock on they send digits. keycode (built next to tpu) shows what
  yours sends. The default bindings cover that keypad:

      7 Home  START OF LINE   GOLD: TOP
      8 Up    MOVE UP         GOLD: TOP
      9 PgUp  PREVIOUS SCREEN
      4 Left  MOVE LEFT       GOLD: PREVIOUS WORD
      5 Begin SELECT          GOLD: RESET
      6 Right MOVE RIGHT      GOLD: NEXT WORD
      1 End   END OF LINE     GOLD: BOTTOM
      2 Down  MOVE DOWN       GOLD: BOTTOM
      3 PgDn  NEXT SCREEN
      0 Ins   INSERT HERE     GOLD: STORE TEXT
      . Del   REMOVE          GOLD: STORE TEXT
      Enter   RETURN          GOLD: DO
      /       text            GOLD: HELP
      *       text            GOLD: SHOW KEYS
      -       text            GOLD: REVERSE
      +       text            GOLD: FORWARD

  GOLD is Esc: press it, release, press the next key.

1 FORWARD
  Set the direction for FIND, MOVE BY LINE and MOVE BY WORD. Key: KP4.

1 REVERSE
  Set the direction to reverse. Key: KP5.

1 CHANGE MODE
  Toggle between insert and overstrike. Keys: Ctrl-A.

1 ERASE WORD
  Erase from the cursor to the end of the word and the spaces after it;
  RESTORE brings it back. Keys: Ctrl-J (Linefeed).

1 ERASE LINE
  Erase the whole current line; RESTORE (GOLD-KP-) brings it back.
  Keys: KP-, GOLD-KP2.

1 ERASE START OF LINE
  Erase from the start of the line to the cursor. Key: Ctrl-U.

1 REFRESH
  Re-read the screen size and repaint. The editor follows terminal
  resizes on its own; REFRESH is for a serial line that cannot signal
  them. Key: Ctrl-W.

1 HELP
  HELP [topic]

  Without a topic: the introduction and the list of topics. With one:
  that topic; any unambiguous abbreviation is accepted. Below the text
  the "Topic?" prompt takes the next topic; Return alone goes back to
  the list, Return at the list leaves; Ctrl-Z leaves at once. The
  navigation keys scroll. HELP KEYS is the live key table, the same
  as SHOW KEYS. Keys: F1, GOLD-H, GOLD-?, GOLD-/.

1 HINTS
  Show or hide the key hint line above the message line: the key in
  reverse video, the command next to it, as many as fit the width.
  The keys are the current ones, DEFINE KEY is reflected at once.

1 KEY FILES
  The key table can live in a text file. At start the editor reads
  ~/.tpu.keys, then ./tpu.keys, then the file named by -keys or by
  the TPU_KEYS environment variable, each over the previous one.
  LOAD KEYS reads a file at any time, SAVE KEYS writes the live table.

  One line per binding, "!" starts a comment:

      KP/, Esc                   = GOLD
      GOLD-Left                  = PREVIOUS WORD
      F6                         = KEYS "ab{Return}{GOLD-KP1}"
      GOLD-k                     = NONE       ! unbinds

  Key names are those of SHOW KEYS (F1, KP7, Insert Here, Begin,
  Ctrl-K, Delete, Space, Return, Tab, Escape, a letter, #hex for
  anything else), several keys through a comma, a blank on both sides
  of "=". KEYS "..." is a learned sequence: text as itself, special
  keys in braces, \" \\ \{ escaped. NONE removes the binding.
  A bad line is reported with its number and skipped, the rest of the
  file is still loaded.

1 LOAD KEYS
  LOAD KEYS [file]

  Read a key file over the current bindings. See HELP KEY FILES.

1 SAVE KEYS
  SAVE KEYS [file]

  Write the current bindings, learned sequences included, as a key
  file; tpu.keys in the current directory by default. Edit it and
  LOAD KEYS, or leave it there: it is read at the next start.

1 KEYS
  Type HELP KEYS or SHOW KEYS to list the current key bindings.
  Keys: GOLD-K, GOLD-F1.

1 SHOW KEYS
  List every key with the command or learned sequence bound to it,
  as the table is right now. Press Return to come back.
  Keys: GOLD-K, GOLD-F1. HELP KEYS shows the same list.

1 WILDCARD FIND
  WILDCARD FIND pattern

  Find the next line that matches a VMS-style pattern as a whole:
  * stands for any string, % for any single character, so "*foo*"
  finds a line containing foo and "%%%:*" a line with a colon in the
  fourth column. A pattern in lower case ignores case. The cursor goes
  to the start of the line.

1 RECALL
  In any prompt the Up and Down arrows (or Ctrl-B) recall previous
  answers; Delete erases, Ctrl-U clears the line, Ctrl-Z cancels.

1 MESSAGES
  Messages look like VMS condition messages:

      %TPU-E-NOTFOUND, could not find: foo

  The letter is the severity (S, I, W, E, F), the name identifies the
  message in the catalogue (tpumsg.h). Every message is also appended
  to the MESSAGES buffer: BUFFER MESSAGES shows the whole history.
